{"name":"Daniel Boone","hex":"#5e4235","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"daniel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The buttocks."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/daniel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Daniel Boone","blocks":[{"text":"Daniel Boone was an American pioneer and frontiersman whose adventures made him one of the first folk heroes of the United States. He became famous for his exploration and settlement of Kentucky, which was then beyond the western borders of the Thirteen Colonies. In 1775, under commission from Judge Richard Henderson's Transylvania Company, Boone blazed the Wilderness Road through the Cumberland Gap and into Kentucky in the face of resistance from nearby Native American tribes. He founded Boonesborough, one of the first English-speaking settlements west of the Appalachian Mountains. By the end of the 18th century, more than 200,000 people had entered Kentucky by following the route marked by Boone."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Chester_Harding_-_Daniel_Boone_-_NPG.2015.102_-_National_Portrait_Gallery_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Chester_Harding_-_Daniel_Boone_-_NPG.2015.102_-_National_Portrait_Gallery_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daniel_Boone"},{"title":"Daniel Boone (1964 TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Daniel Boone is an American action-adventure television series, starring Fess Parker as the frontiersman Daniel Boone, that aired from September 24, 1964, to May 7, 1970, on NBC for 165 episodes, and was produced by 20th Century Fox Television, Arcola Enterprises, and Fespar Corp. Ed Ames co-starred as Mingo, Boone's Cherokee friend, for the first four seasons of the series. Albert Salmi portrayed Boone's companion Yadkin in season one only. Country Western singer-actor Jimmy Dean was a featured actor as Josh Clements during the 1968–1970 seasons. Actor and former NFL football player Rosey Grier made regular appearances as Gabe Cooper in the 1969 to 1970 season. The show was broadcast \"in living color\" beginning in fall 1965, the second season, and was shot entirely in California and Kanab, Utah. The show was highly fictionalized with very little historical accuracy."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9b/Daniel_boone-show.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daniel_Boone_(1964_TV_series)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Boone%27s_First_View_of_Kentucky.jpg/500px-Boone%27s_First_View_of_Kentucky.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Boone%27s_First_View_of_Kentucky.jpg/500px-Boone%27s_First_View_of_Kentucky.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Boone's First View of Kentucky, William Tylee Ranney (1849)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABoone's_First_View_of_Kentucky.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/George_Caleb_Bingham_-_Daniel_Boone_escorting_settlers_through_the_Cumberland_Gap.jpg/500px-George_Caleb_Bingham_-_Daniel_Boone_escorting_settlers_through_the_Cumberland_Gap.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/George_Caleb_Bingham_-_Daniel_Boone_escorting_settlers_through_the_Cumberland_Gap.jpg/1280px-George_Caleb_Bingham_-_Daniel_Boone_escorting_settlers_through_the_Cumberland_Gap.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/George_Caleb_Bingham_-_Daniel_Boone_escorting_settlers_through_the_Cumberland_Gap.jpg/500px-George_Caleb_Bingham_-_Daniel_Boone_escorting_settlers_through_the_Cumberland_Gap.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"George Caleb Bingham's Daniel Boone Escorting Settlers through the Cumberland Gap (1851–52) is a famous depiction of Boone.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGeorge_Caleb_Bingham_-_Daniel_Boone_escorting_settlers_through_the_Cumberland_Gap.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Carl_Wimar_Abduction_of_Boones_Daughter_detail_Amon_Carter_Museum.jpg/500px-Carl_Wimar_Abduction_of_Boones_Daughter_detail_Amon_Carter_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Carl_Wimar_Abduction_of_Boones_Daughter_detail_Amon_Carter_Museum.jpg/1280px-Carl_Wimar_Abduction_of_Boones_Daughter_detail_Amon_Carter_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Carl_Wimar_Abduction_of_Boones_Daughter_detail_Amon_Carter_Museum.jpg/500px-Carl_Wimar_Abduction_of_Boones_Daughter_detail_Amon_Carter_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Abduction of Boone's Daughter, painting by Karl Ferdinand Wimar, 1855, Amon Carter Museum of American Art","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACarl_Wimar_Abduction_of_Boones_Daughter_detail_Amon_Carter_Museum.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Boone_adoption.png/500px-Boone_adoption.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Boone_adoption.png/500px-Boone_adoption.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Illustration of Boone's ritual adoption by the Shawnee, from Life & Times of Col. Daniel Boone, by Cecil B. Hartley (1859)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABoone_adoption.png"},{"srcset":"https: